nd suggested a volunteer program where engineers teach STEM to underprivileged kids.\n  - local schools/centers: Jolene is planning to team up with local schools/centers to conduct workshops and invite engineers as guest speakers, according to a recent discussion with Deborah.\n  - career options: Jolene is considering teaming up with local schools/centers to host workshops and invite engineers as guest speakers to discuss career options with children.\n  - plan: Deborah inquired about Jolene's plan to share knowledge from her mini retreat; Jolene has not solidified the plan yet but is eager to start reaching out.\n  - engineering student: Jolene, an engineering student, wants to use her talents to do good, solve important problems, be innovative, and improve efficiency, influenced by her mother's value of helping others.\n  - communities in need: Jolene wants to make a positive impact on communities in need by creating sustainable solutions through her engineering work.\n  - engineering skills: Jolene's engineering skills led to a major milestone accomplishment last week, making a positive difference for people in a rural area.\n  - engineering project: Jolene wants her engineering projects to use her talents to solve important problems, inspired by her mom stressing helping others. She focuses on new ideas and efficiency.\n  - colleagues: Jolene is an intern at a well-known engineering firm and shared a message mentioning her new colleagues.\n  - engineering professor: Jolene's engineering professor assigned a tough but fun robotics project requiring creativity and problem-solving.\n  - career aspirations: Deborah asked Jolene about her career aspirations after Jolene shared a major engineering accomplishment last week.\n  - problem-solving skills: Jolene is working on a huge, tough but fun robotics project from her engineering professor that is testing her creative and problem-solving skills.\n  - engineer: Jolene, an engineer, is coping with an emotional rollerc